    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_privacy_boundary_pattern","label":"Privacy boundary: LLM sees schema, never raw data","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§2.1","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Every structured-data engagement (Gordian, FinFrock, Axiom) independently converged on the same architecture so a tenant's raw dataset is never sent to the LLM, only schema/column descriptions/metadata; the model generates an operation (SQL or Pandas expression) that a deterministic runtime executes against the real data outside the model. Valuable commercially, not just technically: it lets InApp tell a client 'your dataset is not passed to the LLM.'"},

    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_generate_then_execute_pattern","label":"Generate-then-execute pattern","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§2.2","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Gordian (Pandas), FinFrock (SQL) and Axiom (SQL) all have the model emit a program rather than an answer; a deterministic runtime produces the number. Chosen because it eliminates arithmetic hallucination and makes every answer reproducible and auditable."},

    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_schema_metadata_catalogue_gap","label":"Schema metadata catalogue gap (Phase 1.1)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§4, §5 Phase 1.1","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"WHITELISTED_TABLES is a hardcoded Python dict of bare column names with no descriptions, relationships, per-tenant indexing, or search — identified as the single biggest structural gap versus Axiom's per-tenant indexed schema catalogue approach, and named as the highest-leverage fix because it unblocks confidence scoring, tabular ingestion, and the external-database connector."},

    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_gordian_engagement","label":"Gordian engagement (proposals, RBAC, Pandas + graphs)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§1.1","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null},

    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_axiom_engagement","label":"Axiom engagement (multi-tenant schema-aware SQL at scale)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§1.4","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null},

    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_finfrock_engagement","label":"FinFrock engagement (deterministic text-to-SQL)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§1.3","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null},

    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_external_tenant_db_connector","label":"External tenant database connector (Phase 3.3)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§5 Phase 3.3","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Generalise the SQL path into a connector pointing at a tenant's own database rather than only the platform schema. Highest ceiling and largest security surface in the set, deliberately gated to start only after the schema metadata catalogue (1.1) and query validation/confidence scoring (1.2) are proven in production on the platform's own schema."},

    {"id":"docs_enterprise_chatbot_porting_findings_integration_plan","label":"Integration plan (Phase 1/2/3 ordering)","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\Enterprise-Chatbot-Porting-Findings.md","source_location":"§5","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Ordered by (value x fit with what the platform already has) / effort, so each item plugs into an existing seam (ToolRegistry, pending_clarification, SQL path) rather than requiring the chat graph to be re-architected."},

    {"id":"docs_kt_handover_guide_nerplatform_overview","label":"NER Platform KT Handover Guide","file_type":"document","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\KT-Handover-Guide.html","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null},

    {"id":"docs_kt_handover_guide_bug_wrong_model_served","label":"Six chained bugs: extraction silently used base model","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\KT-Handover-Guide.html","source_location":"§9 Bugs & Issues","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"S3 artifact path mismatch, label_list never persisted, hardcoded port pointing to host-mapped training_service:8003 instead of Docker-internal :8000, missing NER_MODEL_SERVING_URL env var, ONNX input mismatch (token_type_ids sent but not declared), and a too-tight 30s warmup timeout all independently caused inference to fall back to the base model silently, because graceful-degradation fallback code masked every one of them instead of surfacing an error."},

    {"id":"docs_kt_handover_guide_bug_training_quality_f1_zero","label":"Open bug: eval F1/precision/recall = 0.0 on model v8","file_type":"concept","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\KT-Handover-Guide.html","source_location":"§9 Bugs & Issues","source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null},

    {"id":"docs_ner_platform_bundled_page","label":"NER Platform.html (bundled/generated placeholder page)","file_type":"document","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\NER Platform.html","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null},

    {"id":"docs_adr_001_tenant_data_isolation_tenant_data_isolation","label":"ADR-001: Tenant Data Isolation via Separate Database Schemas","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\001-tenant-data-isolation.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Chose separate PostgreSQL schema per tenant (tenant_<uuid>) over (a) separate database per tenant (strongest isolation but high per-database operational cost) and (c) shared tables with row-level security (lowest overhead but highest misconfiguration/leakage risk). Schema-per-tenant gives strong logical isolation without per-database overhead, single migration script set, and single connection pool, at the cost of a shared-fate single PostgreSQL instance and linear schema-count scaling requiring sharding above ~500 tenants."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_002_base_model_strategy_single_curated_base_model","label":"ADR-002: Single Curated Base Model Strategy (No BYOM)","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\002-base-model-strategy.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Chose a single curated base model (dslim/bert-base-NER) for every tenant over (b) a curated multi-model list or (c) full bring-your-own-model, to eliminate the model compatibility matrix, keep a single ONNX export/optimization path, and make cross-tenant metric comparisons meaningful because baselines are identical — at the cost of blocking tenants with existing fine-tuned models and requiring re-fine-tuning all tenants on any base model upgrade."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_003_model_serving_topology_shared_serving_layer","label":"ADR-003: Per-Tenant Model Serving Topology (Shared Pool + Routing)","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\003-model-serving-topology.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Chose a shared Model Serving Layer with per-tenant routing and version pinning (with a path to dedicated pods for high-volume tenants) over (a) a dedicated inference pod per tenant (strongest isolation, high cost for low-volume tenants) and (c) serverless per-request inference (lowest cost but multi-minute cold-start latency), to amortize GPU utilization across tenants while accepting a noisy-neighbor risk mitigated by per-tenant rate limiting, QoS weights, and dedicated-pod escalation."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_004_openspec_governance_openspec_sdd_governance","label":"ADR-004: OpenSpec Spec-Driven Development Governance","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\004-openspec-governance.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Adopted OpenSpec SDD with mandatory proposal/design/spec/tasks/evidence/archive gates over (a) ad-hoc documentation (no traceability enforcement) and (c) fully formalized specification with formal verification (unsustainable overhead), because the project mandates AI-native delivery traceable from business intent through implementation evidence across multiple concurrent human and agent contributors; a documented exception list keeps trivial changes low-overhead."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_005_opencode_agent_boundaries_role_specific_agents","label":"ADR-005: OpenCode Agent Permissions and Boundaries","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\005-opencode-agent-boundaries.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Chose role-specific agents with bounded tool access and human approval gates over (a) a single general-purpose agent (high risk of unintended cross-domain actions) and (c) a fully automated agent pipeline (fastest but highest risk), to prevent e.g. a coding agent from modifying infrastructure config or a QA agent deploying to production, at the cost of more agent handoffs for simple changes."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_006_training_infrastructure_celery_gpu_workers","label":"ADR-006: Training Infrastructure with Asynchronous GPU Workers","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\006-training-infrastructure.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Chose a hybrid of (b) Celery-based async GPU workers with a RabbitMQ broker and (c) dedicated K8s Job per training run, backed by GPU node pools that autoscale 0->N, over (a) synchronous training in the API process (unacceptable — blocks the API), so training never blocks the API/extraction path while getting mature task management, retries, and per-job monitoring; checkpointing enables resume from failure."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_007_chatbot_architecture_full_rag_pipeline","label":"ADR-007: Chatbot Architecture with Full RAG and Guardrails","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\007-chatbot-architecture.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Chose a full RAG pipeline combining SQL over extracted entities, pgvector semantic/full-text document search, and real-time NER inference, over (a) pure SQL generation (lacks document context/NER) and (c) a fine-tuned LLM per tenant (prohibitive cost/complexity), to deliver rich, citable answers within a P95<10s target; guardrails include SQL validation in read-only transactions, mandatory source citation, and query complexity limits."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_008_base_model_as_default_version_zero_default_model","label":"ADR-008: Base Model as Default Inference Model (Version 0)","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\008-base-model-as-default.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Treats dslim/bert-base-NER as a synthetic 'version 0' for every tenant so extraction requests never 404 before a tenant completes annotation->training->promotion; chosen over leaving the prior 404/400 behavior in place, which rendered the platform unusable until first promotion. Cost: base-model predictions only cover CoNLL labels, not tenant-specific ones, until a fine-tuned version is promoted."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_009_system_admin_sets_training_hyperparameters_admin_set_hyperparams","label":"ADR-009: System Admin Sets Training Hyperparameters at Approval","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\009-system-admin-sets-training-hyperparameters.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Moved hyperparameter selection (learning_rate, num_epochs, batch_size, max_seq_length) from Tenant Admin submission time to System Admin approval time, because under ADR-006's original design the System Admin approval was a rubber-stamp enqueuing whatever the tenant chose, putting GPU-cost- and model-quality-sensitive decisions in the hands of a role without cluster-capacity or training-best-practice visibility. Cost: approval is no longer single-click; requires filling four fields per job."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_010_per_entity_type_dataset_threshold_per_entity_readiness","label":"ADR-010: Dataset Readiness Measured Per Entity Type","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\010-per-entity-type-dataset-threshold.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Replaced ADR-006's self-contradictory 500-entity-total threshold (stated once as tenant-wide, once as per-type, and never actually enforced since NER_MIN_TRAINING_ENTITIES defaulted to 0) with a per-entity-type threshold of 200, evaluated over the union of active entity_definitions and spanned types, capped at 100% per type before averaging — because a tenant-wide total is a poor proxy for trainability: NER quality is bounded by the weakest label, and a tenant with 500 spans concentrated in one type showed 5% dashboard readiness while five configured types had zero spans and were invisible entirely."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_011_tenant_scoped_azure_connection_control_plane_azure_connection_control_plane","label":"ADR-011: Tenant-Scoped Azure Connection Control Plane","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\011-tenant-scoped-azure-connection-control-plane.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Extends the existing integration-profile control plane with tenant-bound lifecycle records in the public schema for Azure Blob and Azure PostgreSQL only, storing secret references (Vault-resolved outside local Compose) and non-sensitive metadata rather than (per Alternatives Considered) developer-managed profiles (can't meet self-service requirements), plaintext credential/config storage (violates secret-management controls), or arbitrary provider/connection-string input (cannot be safely validated as a finite catalog). Requires TLS, least-privilege read-only credentials, and a passing safe connection test plus governance approval before activation."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_012_durable_azure_blob_source_synchronization_durable_blob_sync","label":"ADR-012: Durable Azure Blob Source Synchronization","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\012-durable-azure-blob-source-synchronization.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Uses a contained Azure Blob runtime plus Celery/RabbitMQ source-sync jobs with a tenant-bound object/version ledger and lease for idempotency, over (per Alternatives Considered) keeping in-process asyncio dispatch (loses scheduled work on restart, no durable catch-up) or building Azure-specific OCR/chunking/extraction paths (duplicates the common pipeline) or persisting Blob originals durably in MinIO (violates source-only retention) — so manual, 15-minute scheduled, retry, and missed-schedule catch-up synchronization all share one durable, observable job that reuses existing NormalizedDocument/DocumentIngestionService ingestion and deletes temporary Blob bytes on every terminal path."},

    {"id":"docs_adr_013_contract_governed_external_postgresql_chat_contract_governed_external_sql","label":"ADR-013: Contract-Governed External PostgreSQL Chat","file_type":"rationale","source_file":"C:\\Users\\Arjun.jayakumar\\Documents\\GitHub\\ner-project\\docs\\adr\\013-contract-governed-external-postgresql-chat.md","source_location":null,"source_url":null,"captured_at":null,"author":null,"contributor":null,"rationale":"Introduces a separate tenant-bound external-query capability validating a versioned canonical JSON schema contract and join graph, with a live schema fingerprint checked before every query and blocking on drift, executed as one AST-validated parameterized SELECT under a Vault-held least-privilege read-only role with a row cap and 10s timeout — chosen over (per Alternatives Considered) copying business rows into platform storage (violates no-copy requirement), using vector similarity to authorize schema (not deterministic authorization), reusing the platform SQL whitelist unchanged (doesn't represent customer schema/credentials), or permitting unrestricted read-only SQL (can still expose unauthorized data or exhaust resources). The LLM never holds database credentials and result rows are never persisted."}
  ],

